implementationPlan.note:
RESUME session. The previous CLI (cli-exit=1) wrote scripts/local-runner/guest-session.mjs, checkpointed in 750921e. I re-verified it on disk against every step-2 clause rather than trusting the dead session.
- runGuestSession validates its whole input before spawn, each check failing as SESSION_INPUT_INVALID:
  - sessionId is nonblank, at most 255 UTF-16 units, with no NUL/CR/LF, and is kept verbatim;
  - the callbacks and spawn must be functions, and the signal an AbortSignal;
  - executable uses multipass.mjs's convention (string, nonblank, no control characters);
  - daemonInfoPath must be absolute with no control characters, and timeoutMs a safe integer from 1 to 60000;
  - the binding is snapshotted and frozen, then checked through scopeRequest on a synthetic get_context, which is never dispatched;
  - bridgeArgv(name) is used unchanged.
- daemon.json read:
  - open uses O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK (0 where unsupported) and requires a regular file;
  - it reads at most 16 KiB plus a one-byte overflow sentinel and always closes the handle;
  - missing, unreadable, malformed or non-object content is SESSION_DAEMON_INFO_INVALID;
  - only an absent bindHost or a literal 127.0.0.1/::1 passes; anything else is SESSION_DAEMON_NOT_LOOPBACK.
- The guard runs before spawn and again before every forward.
- spawn(executable, bridgeArgv, {shell:false, windowsHide:true, stdio: three pipes}). Hello must come first and once; request ids must strictly increase, tracked by one lastId; exactly one observation is allowed.
- Output goes only to onOutput, and stderr is drained unread.
- The session resolves {observation, executionReady:false} only after a clean decoder EOF, provider close code 0, one observation and no owed reply.
Evidence (Linux node v24.21.0, python3 3.12.3):
- bridge file: 34/34 pass, H1-H4 included;
- baseline of the full four-file gate: exit 0, 247 pass, 1 skip (the macOS native smoke).
Size note: so far +239 (session) and +253/-3 (tests). The 400-net target will be exceeded once step 4 lands; that is handled at completion.
The 551 dirty paths from other sessions are untouched.

Adversarial self-review over the two owned paths (numstat vs 750921e~1: guest-session.mjs +238/-0, bridge test +343/-3).

Findings fixed in this step:
- The hello deadline is the only deadline, but its cancellation once hello arrives was untested. An H4 row now covers it: a job runs 3x timeoutMs and still settles with its observation.
- The fake kill now closes asynchronously, as in Node. A synchronous close masked the `failure ??= 'SESSION_TIMEOUT'` guard. A forced-kill row covers it: SESSION_TIMEOUT, kills ['SIGKILL'], no observation.
- The H2 replay, abort-listener and decoder-end checks were rebuilt, because owed replies, the once-listener and a missing observation each masked a mutant.
- Four of my lines over 150 characters were wrapped (formatting only).

Mutation evidence: 55 mutants, 54 killed. The step-6 set (8/8 killed):
- startup daemon guard removed;
- abort precheck removed;
- get_context stamped with the execution trio;
- hello not cancelled;
- failure overwritten;
- executionReady true;
- lastId not advanced;
- stop not idempotent.
Earlier, the step-4 e2e set killed no-scope, no-stamp, output-dispatched and no-stdin-close. The one survivor, no-isFile, is equivalent on Linux: reading a directory fails with EISDIR, which is refused anyway.

Checklist covered:
- binding substitution and a caller mutating the binding after start (snapshot);
- settings and dependency tools are TOOL_DENIED end to end;
- nested JSON is depth-bounded both ways;
- replayed and duplicate ids are a violation;
- hello and exit ordering races;
- frame-shaped bytes on both child streams stay output;
- abort, timeout or close overlapping a late forward or a backpressured write;
- the daemon bindHost is re-checked per request;
- no retry, a pending bound of 64, no buffered output, and no leaked fd, timer or listener;
- executionReady is always false.

Theoretical or downstream:
- The daemon-guard TOCTOU between the check and the forward.
- A supervisor killed by a signal leaves its start_new_session job alive. This was observed locally when a mutant run's supervisor was SIGKILLed: the orphan was reparented to init and swept. That is R10/R11 territory.
- Real multipass/sudo EOF propagation and guest-to-host /mcp and /ws probes belong to R13.

